From b35aa7b2dac5782f353801d74352c0109c98a653 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:52:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 17/19] feat(mcp): validate tool arguments against the input
 schema; refuse unknown arguments (C30)

The server registers tools through the go-sdk's raw AddTool path, which
does not validate arguments against the declared input schema (the
repository notes this in pkg/toolsets/netobserv/tools/schema.go). Each
handler checks what it reads; anything else is ignored. On the probes,
`dryRun: true` (no tool has it) was ignored and the write happened, and
several handlers dropped wrongly typed values (K8S-09; the fixed build
refuses the three that were found). Same root cause as #1584.

Every tool call is now checked before its handler runs:
- an argument the schema does not declare is refused, and the error
  lists the arguments the tool accepts;
- the declared types, enums, patterns, minimums and nested object
  shapes are enforced, with the schema validator's message;
- required arguments are left to the handlers, which already report
  them with their own messages (unchanged).
Nothing is done for a refused call; the result is a tool error, not a
protocol error. Tools whose schema allows additional properties at the
top level would need that respected; none does today.

Tests that asserted a handler's own message for a value the schema
already rejects (a string for a boolean, a negative limit, an unknown
enum value, an empty kind) now assert the validator's message: the call
still fails, earlier and with the schema's wording. One nodes_log test
passed `tail`, which nodes_log does not have (its argument is
`tailLines`) and which was silently ignored; it now passes
`tailLines: 0` (all lines), the value it meant.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
